WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.300
How do Cook Levin constraints

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.900
prevent a fake computation? We

3
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.320
will use the course’s four

4
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.580
indicator families and explain all

5
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.680
eight groups of conditions. The

6
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.980
goal is a valid accepting

7
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.580
bounded machine run, not merely

8
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.080
a colourful grid that happens

9
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.000
to contain an accepting-state symbol.

10
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.340
The initial-configuration group fixes the

11
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.220
start state, initial head cell,

12
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.340
input symbols and all remaining

13
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:27.440
blank cells. Without these facts,

14
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.400
a satisfying tableau might describe

15
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.620
a machine started on the

16
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.260
wrong input or in a

17
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.740
helpful later configuration. The formula

18
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.300
is supposed to answer membership

19
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.100
for this particular input x.

20
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.520
The next groups require exactly

21
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.380
one state per time and

22
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.380
exactly one symbol in every

23
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.440
cell at every time. Exactly

24
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.540
one means at least one,

25
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.120
plus pairwise clauses forbidding two

26
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.940
simultaneously true choices. It is

27
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.080
not an exclusive-or parity condition,

28
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.820
which would allow three true

29
00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:58.400
choices.

30
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.160
Head uniqueness requires one and

31
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.720
only one head position in

32
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.740
every configuration. An at-least-one head

33
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.180
clause prevents no-head rows, and

34
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.180
the pairwise negative clauses prevent

35
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.160
a machine from simultaneously reading

36
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.820
several distant cells. The head-position

37
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.420
indicators are separate from the

38
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.140
state and tape-symbol indicators in

39
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:18.980
the course’s encoding.

40
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.100
Acceptance requires an accepting state

41
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.520
at some configuration time, including

42
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.260
time zero if immediate acceptance

43
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.360
is possible. Instruction uniqueness chooses

44
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.100
one machine instruction at each

45
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.180
transition time, not after the

46
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.580
final configuration. For early halting,

47
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.820
normalize the bounded encoding with

48
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.260
a consistent absorbing continuation instead

49
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.340
of accidentally requiring an ordinary

50
00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.720
halted machine to move.

51
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.060
Non-head cells must keep their

52
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.480
tape contents. A convenient clause

53
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.540
says that if the head

54
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.560
is not here and this

55
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.660
cell contains b now, it

56
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.380
still contains b next time.

57
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.740
In conjunctive normal form this

58
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.980
is head-here or not-current-b or

59
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.420
next-b, together with the unique-symbol

60
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:01.860
conditions.

61
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.980
The valid-transition group ties the

62
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.620
chosen instruction to the correct

63
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.200
old state and read symbol.

64
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.100
It also enforces the new

65
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.700
state, the written symbol at

66
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.660
the head cell and the

67
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.760
new head position. All these

68
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.140
implications are needed; checking only

69
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.440
the write would still permit

70
00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.140
an impossible state change or

71
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:20.600
head jump.

72
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.480
Use the actual tape-boundary convention

73
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.160
when writing the new-head condition.

74
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:26.760
A left move at the

75
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.280
left end must not refer

76
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.120
to an undefined cell zero

77
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.180
if the encoding numbers cells

78
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.800
from one. Likewise, a constraint

79
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.000
mentioning time i plus one

80
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.960
must stop at i equal

81
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.780
to T minus one, not

82
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.840
create an unencoded T plus

83
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.600
one row.

84
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:45.560
For a fixed machine, the

85
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.020
numbers of states, symbols and

86
00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.400
possible instructions are constants. With

87
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.900
order T times and order

88
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.580
T cells, the indicator count

89
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.120
is order T squared. However,

90
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.160
pairwise head uniqueness uses order

91
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.380
T squared pairs at each

92
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.400
time, yielding order T cubed

93
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.220
such clauses over all rows.

94
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.380
The at-least-one head clauses also

95
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.880
have length proportional to the

96
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.620
number of cells, so not

97
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.560
every displayed clause has constant

98
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.320
length. Including written variable indices

99
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.520
adds logarithmic representation factors, still

100
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.780
leaving a polynomial-size formula because

101
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.520
T itself is polynomial in

102
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.460
input length. The theorem survives;

103
00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.720
the raw deck’s blanket quadratic

104
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.220
constant-clause summary does not describe

105
00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.020
this literal expansion accurately.

106
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.800
For the forward correctness direction,

107
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.900
fill every indicator from a

108
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.960
legal padded accepting branch and

109
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.020
check each constraint group. For

110
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.980
the reverse, uniqueness gives actual

111
00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.380
rows, initialization gives the right

112
00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.480
input, and transition plus unchanged-cell

113
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.160
conditions give a legal run.

114
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.840
Acceptance then identifies a successful

115
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.200
branch, completing the required if

116
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.400
and only if.

117
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.960
Things to take note of.

118
00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.620
Explain each constraint’s purpose and

119
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.700
a fake tableau it forbids,

120
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.840
rather than memorizing an overbar-sensitive

121
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.900
formula without meaning. Keep row

122
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.880
and transition indices consistent, define

123
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.940
padding and boundaries, and count

124
00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.880
the particular encoding you use;

125
00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.500
polynomial correctness matters more than

126
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.160
an unsupported tight exponent.
